--TEST--
`Core\Response::bytes` names the last content type a header field can carry and the first one it cannot, and refuses with nothing written -- `rule:security/sink-predicate` and `rule:security/response-body-is-one-typed-member` and `rule:http-server/an-unsafe-or-unbounded-default-is-a-defect`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `rule:security/sink-predicate`: the content type is a sink, because it is the one string here
// that becomes an *instruction* to the peer about how to read the rest. So the
// bound is asserted on both sides -- a member that stopped one byte early
// would print plausibly against either half alone.
//
// Accepted: printable ASCII, from the narrowest media type to one carrying
// every punctuation character a parameter list uses.
array<string> $spellable = [
    "a/b",
    "application/vnd.api+json",
    "text/plain; charset=utf-8",
    "multipart/form-data; boundary=\"~!#$%&'*+-.^_`|\"",
];
uint $accepted = 0;
foreach ($spellable as string $type) {
    try {
        Core\Response::bytes("" as bytes, $type);
        $accepted = $accepted + 1;
    } catch (LogicError $refused) {
    }
}
echo "accepted ", $accepted, " of 4\n";

// Refused: empty, and every byte outside a field value -- the newline that is
// header injection, the carriage return beside it, a control character, and a
// non-ASCII byte that no field value may carry.
array<string> $unspellable = [
    "",
    "text/plain\r\nX-Injected: yes",
    "text/plain\nX-Injected: yes",
    "text/pl\tain",
    "text/plänn",
];
uint $refusedCount = 0;
foreach ($unspellable as string $type) {
    try {
        Core\Response::bytes("body" as bytes, $type);
    } catch (LogicError $refused) {
        $refusedCount = $refusedCount + 1;
    }
}
echo "refused ", $refusedCount, " of 5\n";

// The sentence one of them refuses with, which names what it was given rather
// than only that something was wrong. The empty type is the one whose message
// is spellable in an exact expectation.
try {
    Core\Response::bytes("body" as bytes, "");
} catch (LogicError $empty) {
    echo $empty->message, "\n";
}

// And the refusal wrote nothing: a body sent under a header the server then
// could not spell is the failure this check exists to prevent.
// The `catch` inside the callable binds `$injected` rather than `$refused`:
// repeating an enclosing frame's `catch` name inside a callable is an ICE in
// the lowerer today, which the playbook's *Writing a test case* section owns.
var $written = Core\Out::capture(fn (): void => {
    try {
        Core\Response::bytes("body" as bytes, "text/plain\r\nX-Injected: yes");
    } catch (LogicError $injected) {
    }
});
echo "wrote [", $written, "]\n";
--EXPECT--
accepted 4 of 4
refused 5 of 5
Core\Response::bytes(): `` is not a media type a `Content-Type` header can carry — a field value is printable ASCII and never empty
wrote []
